\title{A Contrarian Fixed-Point Analysis of Causal Loops \\ and the Novikov Self-Consistency Principle}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-14}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a compact mathematical theory of closed timelike curves in which a causal
loop is modelled by its one-traversal evolution map $\evolve : X \to X$ on a space $X$
of world-states, and the Novikov self-consistency principle is identified with the
existence of a fixed point. Under this dictionary a \emph{consistent history} is a
solution of $\evolve(x) = x$, and the \emph{consistency count} is the number of such
solutions. Rather than only establishing existence of consistent histories, we take a
deliberately contrarian stance and test a battery of natural structural hypotheses
that appear to force self-consistency. We prove that three of them fail --
reversibility (bijectivity), descent along repetition, and closure under composition --
by exhibiting explicit minimal counterexamples. We then isolate the hypotheses that
genuinely compel consistency: consistency ascends under repetition; a contracting loop
on a complete state space has a \emph{unique} consistent history (via the Banach
fixed-point theorem); an involutive loop on a finite state space satisfies the parity
congruence $c(\evolve) \equiv |X| \pmod 2$, whence odd-sized worlds always admit an
odd (hence positive) number of consistent histories; and any loop on a finite
non-empty world becomes consistent after some positive number of repetitions. We close
with concrete counts and a program of extensions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The grandfather paradox is the canonical objection to backward time travel: an agent
travels to the past and prevents its own existence, generating a contradiction. The
\emph{Novikov self-consistency principle} resolves the objection not by forbidding
time travel but by forbidding \emph{inconsistent} time travel: the only physically
realizable histories along a closed timelike curve are those that are globally
self-consistent. Informally, the universe admits only those scripts that close up on
themselves.

Our aim is to give this principle a spare, exact mathematical formulation and then to
interrogate it. The formulation is elementary: a causal loop is a self-map
$\evolve : X \to X$ of the space $X$ of complete world-states, and a self-consistent
history is a fixed point. What makes the subject rich is not this translation but the
\emph{contrarian} questions it invites. Existence theorems are only half of the story;
the sharper question is which structural features of a loop \emph{force} consistency
and which merely appear to. We assemble a list of plausible ``forcing'' hypotheses,
and for each we either supply a proof or destroy it with an explicit counterexample.
The resulting map of the territory is asymmetric and, we think, genuinely surprising.

\section{The model}

Throughout, $X$ denotes a set (the \emph{state space}), whose elements are complete
world-states.

\begin{definition}[Causal loop]
A \emph{causal loop} on $X$ is a function $\evolve : X \to X$. The value $\evolve(x)$
is the world-state produced by feeding the state $x$ once around the loop.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Self-consistency / Novikov principle]
A causal loop $\evolve$ is \emph{self-consistent} if it possesses a fixed point:
\[
  \exists\, x \in X, \quad \evolve(x) = x.
\]
Such an $x$ is a \emph{consistent history}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Consistency count]
If $X$ is finite, the \emph{consistency count} of $\evolve$ is
\[
  c(\evolve) := \bigl|\{\, x \in X : \evolve(x) = x \,\}\bigr|.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Count detects consistency]
If $c(\evolve) > 0$ then $\evolve$ is self-consistent.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
A non-empty finite set of fixed points contains an element, which is by definition a
consistent history.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Running examples.}
Two loops recur throughout. The \emph{grandfather loop} takes
$X = \{\mathsf{T}, \mathsf{F}\}$ and $\evolve(x) = \lnot x$ (logical negation, which
swaps the two states). The \emph{identity loop} takes any $X$ and $\evolve(x) = x$.

\section{Disproofs: hypotheses that do not force consistency}

\begin{theorem}[Reversibility is insufficient]
It is not the case that every causal loop on a finite non-empty state space whose
evolution map is a bijection is self-consistent.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The grandfather loop $\evolve(x) = \lnot x$ on $X = \{\mathsf{T},\mathsf{F}\}$ is a
bijection (it is its own inverse), but $\lnot\mathsf{T} = \mathsf{F} \ne \mathsf{T}$
and $\lnot\mathsf{F} = \mathsf{T} \ne \mathsf{F}$, so it has no fixed point.
\end{proof}

Write $\evolve^{[k]}$ for the loop traversed $k$ times (the $k$-fold composition).

\begin{theorem}[Consistency does not descend]
There is a causal loop $\evolve$ such that $\evolve^{[2]}$ is self-consistent but
$\evolve$ is not.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take the grandfather loop. Its double traversal is $\lnot\lnot x = x$, the identity,
consistent everywhere; yet $\evolve$ itself has no fixed point.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Consistency is not compositional]
There exist self-consistent causal loops $f, g$ on a common finite state space whose
composite $f \circ g$ is not self-consistent.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $X = \{0,1,2\}$. Let $f$ swap $0$ and $1$ and fix $2$; then $f(2)=2$, so $f$ is
self-consistent. Let $g$ swap $1$ and $2$ and fix $0$; then $g(0)=0$, so $g$ is
self-consistent. Applying $g$ then $f$,
\[
  0 \mapsto 0 \mapsto 1, \qquad 1 \mapsto 2 \mapsto 2, \qquad 2 \mapsto 1 \mapsto 0,
\]
so $f \circ g$ is the $3$-cycle $0 \to 1 \to 2 \to 0$, which fixes no point.
\end{proof}

\section{Proofs: hypotheses that do force consistency}

\begin{theorem}[Consistency ascends]
If $\evolve$ is self-consistent, then $\evolve^{[k]}$ is self-consistent for every
$k \in \mathbb{N}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $\evolve(x) = x$. By induction on $k$, applying $\evolve$ to a fixed point returns
it unchanged, so $\evolve^{[k]}(x) = x$.
\end{proof}

Equip $X$ with a metric. Recall $\evolve$ is a \emph{contraction with constant}
$K \in [0,1)$ if $d(\evolve(x), \evolve(y)) \le K\, d(x,y)$ for all $x,y$.

\begin{theorem}[Deterministic time travel]
Let $X$ be a non-empty complete metric space and $\evolve : X \to X$ a contraction
with constant $K < 1$. Then $\evolve$ has a unique consistent history.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
This is the Banach fixed-point theorem. Iterating $x_{n+1} = \evolve(x_n)$ from any
$x_0$ yields a Cauchy sequence (since $d(x_{n+1},x_n) \le K^n d(x_1,x_0)$), whose
limit $x^\star$ is a fixed point by continuity. If $\evolve(x)=x$ and $\evolve(y)=y$
then $d(x,y) \le K\,d(x,y)$ with $K<1$ forces $x=y$.
\end{proof}

A loop is \emph{involutive} if $\evolve(\evolve(x)) = x$ for all $x$.

\begin{theorem}[Parity congruence]
Let $X$ be finite and $\evolve$ involutive. Then $c(\evolve) \equiv |X| \pmod 2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Partition $X$ into $\Fix = \{x : \evolve(x)=x\}$ and $\Mov = \{x : \evolve(x)\ne x\}$.
On $\Mov$, $\evolve$ is a fixed-point-free involution, pairing each moved state with a
distinct partner, so $|\Mov|$ is even. Hence
$|X| = |\Fix| + |\Mov| = c(\evolve) + (\text{even})$, so $c(\evolve)$ and $|X|$ share
the same parity.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Odd worlds are always consistent]
If $X$ is finite of odd cardinality and $\evolve$ is involutive, then $c(\evolve)$ is
odd; in particular $\evolve$ is self-consistent.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
$c(\evolve) \equiv |X| \equiv 1 \pmod 2$, so the count is odd and positive.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Eventual consistency]
Let $X$ be finite and non-empty and $\evolve : X \to X$ any causal loop. Then there
exists $k \ge 1$ with $\evolve^{[k]}$ self-consistent.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Fix $x_0$ and consider the orbit $x_0, \evolve(x_0), \evolve^{[2]}(x_0), \dots$. By
finiteness (pigeonhole) there are $i < j$ with $\evolve^{[i]}(x_0) =
\evolve^{[j]}(x_0)$. Put $y = \evolve^{[i]}(x_0)$ and $k = j - i \ge 1$; then
$\evolve^{[k]}(y) = y$.
\end{proof}

\section{Concrete counts}

\begin{proposition}
For the grandfather loop on $\{\mathsf{T},\mathsf{F}\}$, $c(\evolve) = 0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}
For the identity loop on a two-state world, $c(\evolve) = 2$.
\end{proposition}

Both are consistent with the parity law: both loops are involutive on an even-sized
world, and $0 \equiv 2 \equiv 0 \pmod 2$.

\section{Discussion and future work}

Self-consistency of a causal loop is a fixed-point property, and the character of the
loop determines how strongly it is compelled. Weak, appealing hypotheses --
reversibility, descent along repetition, closure under composition -- are
insufficient; specific structure -- ascent under repetition, metric contraction, and
finite-world involutivity -- suffices, the last two even quantitatively, and finiteness
alone forces eventual consistency.

Several directions extend this program: a mod-$p$ congruence
$c(\evolve) \equiv |X| \pmod p$ for loops of prime order $p$; a compositional-repair
theorem for commuting loops; a topological Novikov principle via the Brouwer/Schauder
fixed-point theorems on compact convex state spaces; a probabilistic version in which
causal steps are Markov kernels and consistent histories are stationary distributions;
expected fixed-point counts for random loops; and joint consistency of interacting
loops as a simultaneous fixed-point problem.

\section{Conclusion}

By identifying self-consistent histories with fixed points, the Novikov principle
becomes an ordinary -- and richly structured -- question in fixed-point theory. Our
contrarian survey shows that several intuitively obvious forcing hypotheses fail, while
a precise trio (ascent under repetition, contraction, and finite-world involutivity)
succeeds, the latter two carrying quantitative content. The grandfather paradox,
reframed, is simply the statement that a particular self-map has no fixed point.

